      {
        "key": "react:js_basics",
        "text": "JavaScript executes code in a single-threaded runtime model using a call stack, a heap, and an event loop coordinating macrotask and microtask queues. When asynchronous APIs like Promise callbacks or MutationObservers resolve, their jobs are enqueued onto the microtask queue. The microtask queue is processed completely after the current execution context empties and before the browser yields control back to rendering or the macrotask queue. This architecture prevents UI re-renders during microtask flushes, ensuring consistent intermediate state observation, but can cause main thread starvation if microtasks recursively enqueue additional microtasks without returning control."
      }
